publicly timestamped to the Bitcoin blockchain. Gate 1, the +46.5
headline, is the exception: its 2026-08-12 freeze predates the public
repository and rests on git history we control. Stated here rather than
only in the ledger at the bottom, because it is the headline.
[...]
| 1 | Does adaptation beat the **same model's own 30-shot prompt** on schemas it has never seen? | **GO** | **+46.5 F1** seed-mean over 3 tenant schemas (+36.0/+49.0/+54.4) vs a +5.0 bar frozen beforehand. Sign test 156W/0L/2T — over **158 scored pairs of 180 designed**; 22 were excluded because the 30-shot prompt exceeded the frozen 8192-token budget, disclosed symmetrically. (re-run pending: Addendum Z, preregistered and anchored 2026-09-23, stores every model output; its number replaces this one whichever way it moves, and a non-GO withdraws it — `docs/research/ADDENDUM_Z_PROTOCOL.md`) |
[...]
**And the prompted baseline is much stronger on realistic documents than the headline corpus implies.** On the synthetic novel-schema corpus the same model's 30-shot prompt scores 0.4333–0.6208, which is what makes +46.5 possible. On the freight-waybill corpus a 20-shot prompt scores **0.7836**. The gap the product has to earn is far narrower where the documents look real — measured, not estimated, in the paired run below.
[...]
| 1 (novel schema, k=30) | **0 of 158** | +46.5 | **+46.5** |
[...]
- **The headline is the least verifiable number on this page, and that is worth saying plainly.** Gate 1's arms predate primary-evidence storage: they record a per-receipt `micro_f1` and **no predictions**, so `verify_from_primary.py` cannot re-score +46.5 against regenerated gold — it reports the artifact as unverifiable and does not silently pass it. What can be checked is the arithmetic (`verify_verdict.py` re-aggregates the stored per-receipt scores) and the corpus (the generator is deterministic and in the repo); what cannot is the step from model output to score. Gates 4 and 5 store raw predictions and are primary-verifiable end to end. Combined with the row-1 freeze resting on private git history, **the number this project is named after is the one with the weakest verification path**, and re-running those arms with predictions stored is the fix — it was priced here until 2026-09-23 as ~9 CPU-hours for the prompted arms alone, which was wrong (CORRECTIONS 2026-09-23; by the arithmetic of the cost paragraph in `docs/research/ADDENDUM_Z_PROTOCOL.md`, ~14–24 wall-hours on four cores for those three arms and ~35–57 for all six, quoted there as 40–60; its per-document input is prose only, not banked), and it is preregistered as Addendum Z and not yet landed.
